Sections "PROJECT" contain missmatched items: {'PROJECT_SUMMARY': ["Ecdysteroidogenic cells synthesise and secrete ecdysone, the major developmental hormone of insects. In some insect species ecdysteroidogenic cells form a homogeneous organ called the prothoracic gland. Through a complex series of enzymatic reactions carried out by enzymes encoded by the Halloween genes, cholesterol is converted to ecdysone which is then secreted to orchestrate the developmental programme of ecdysis and metamorphosis. The biosynthetic pathway of ecdysone is based on a proposed model that accounts for the presence of multiple enzymes all expressed exclusively by these cells. Although long considered as synthesizing and secreting only ecdysone, the precise mechanism of ecdysone biosynthesis by the ecdysteroidogenic cells remains unknown. Lipidomicss analysis was carried out on ecdysteroidogenic cells of an insect to identify what lipids, if any, are also secreted by these cells. More precisely, the prothoracic glands (ecdysteroidogenic cells) of the silkworm, Bombyx mori, were isolated on Day 6 of the 5th instar (D-V-6) and three sets of samples were produced. Those Before (termed B) incubation for 2 hrs in Ringer's saline, those After (termed P) incubation for 2 hrs in Ringer's saline and the incubation medium (Ringer’s saline (termed S) after 2 hrs of incubation. All these three sets of samples were done in 3 biological replicates (n=3). A range of 647 lipids were identified to be secreted from these ecdysteroidogenic cells.", "Ecdysteroidogenic cells synthesise and secrete ecdysone, the major developmental hormone of insects. In some insect species ecdysteroidogenic cells form a homogeneous organ called the prothoracic gland. Through a complex series of enzymatic reactions carried out by enzymes encoded by the Halloween genes, cholesterol is converted to ecdysone which is then secreted to orchestrate the developmental programme of ecdysis and metamorphosis. The biosynthetic pathway of ecdysone is based on a proposed model that accounts for the presence of multiple enzymes all expressed exclusively by these cells. Although long considered as synthesizing and secreting only ecdysone, the precise mechanism of ecdysone biosynthesis by the ecdysteroidogenic cells remains unknown. Lipidomicss analysis was carried out on ecdysteroidogenic cells of an insect to identify what lipids, if any, are also secreted by these cells. More precisely, the prothoracic glands (ecdysteroidogenic cells) of the silkworm, Bombyx mori, were isolated on Day 6 of the 5th instar (D-V-6) and three sets of samples were produced. Those Before (termed B) incubation for 2 hrs in Ringer''s saline, those After (termed P) incubation for 2 hrs in Ringer''s saline and the incubation medium (Ringer’s saline (termed S) after 2 hrs of incubation. All these three sets of samples were done in 3 biological replicates (n=3). A range of 647 lipids were identified to be secreted from these ecdysteroidogenic cells."]}
